Subject: [PATCH 2/7] aggregate_copy_forward: fail-closed escape analysis; fix
 span-index ICE
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Root cause of the -O2 SIMD miscompile cluster (23 regression failures):
eliminate_dead_aggregate_field_stores used an ALLOWLIST escape analysis
that only recognized Call/Memcpy/Store-of-pointer as escapes. Intrinsic
instructions were invisible — but their dest_ptr AND args are raw
pointers into stack aggregates (__m128i temporaries). Every store
initializing such an aggregate was therefore 'never read' and deleted:
_mm_add_epi32 computed on zeroed inputs (paddd of 0+0; observed slot
mismatch: stores to 88/96(%rsp), vector loads from 136/152(%rsp)).

A pass deleting stores must be DEFAULT-CLOSED: only instructions whose
memory semantics it models precisely (Load read-range, Store written
pointer, GEP/Copy/Phi derivations) may touch a tracked root without
escaping it. Everything else — intrinsics, calls, memcpy, atomics,
inline asm, terminator operands — now escapes via the canonical
for_each_operand/value_use visitors (no hand-maintained list to rot).

Also: spans indexing guarded by exact length equality (old_spans[ii]
ICE'd on 7 tests whenever a prior pass inserted instructions without
maintaining source_spans — dce.rs already followed this convention);
second aggregate_copy_forward/loop_memory_promote callsites now honor
the same CCC_NO_* gates as the first (a kill switch that only silences
one of two callsites is a debugging trap).

PROOF: _mm_set/_mm_add/_mm_storeu differential vs GCC -O2 now
byte-identical (was 0 0 0 0). Regressions 119->142 passed.
650/650 lib tests.
